Agentic AI Workflow

The agent is designed as a multi-step workflow rather than a single LLM prompt.

Conceptually:

START
  │
  ▼
Planner
  │
  ├───────────────┐
  ▼               ▼
Retrieve SOPs   Retrieve Logs
  │               │
  └───────┬───────┘
          │
          ▼
 Retrieve Equipment History
          │
          ▼
       Synthesis
          │
          ▼
 Structured Validation
          │
          ▼
         END

The workflow separates retrieval, tool execution, reasoning, and response generation, making the behaviour easier to inspect and extend than a single large prompt.

Tool Calling

The agent can retrieve information through dedicated operational tools.

search_sops

Searches the local SOP knowledge base for procedures relevant to the reported symptoms.

Example sources:

pump_overheating_sop.txt
vibration_response_sop.txt
search_maintenance_logs

Retrieves relevant maintenance information that can help determine whether similar problems or interventions have previously occurred.

get_equipment_history

Retrieves historical information associated with the selected asset.

Example:

equipment_history.json

Separating these capabilities into tools allows the agent to retrieve information selectively instead of injecting the entire knowledge base into every prompt.

Retrieval-Augmented Generation

The application uses a RAG-style approach to ground the model in operational knowledge.

Conceptually:

Incident Query
      │
      ▼
Operational Retrieval
      │
      ├── SOPs
      ├── Maintenance Records
      └── Equipment History
      │
      ▼
Relevant Context
      │
      ▼
LLM
      │
      ▼
Grounded Troubleshooting Response

This reduces reliance on the LLM's general knowledge and makes the answer more traceable to the information available to the application.

Structured AI Response

The model does not simply return unrestricted text.

The backend expects a structured response containing fields such as:

{
  "summary": "...",
  "possible_causes": [
    "..."
  ],
  "recommended_checks": [
    "..."
  ],
  "confidence": "Medium",
  "tools_used": [
    "search_sops",
    "get_equipment_history"
  ],
  "sources": [
    "pump_overheating_sop.txt",
    "equipment_history.json"
  ]
}

Pydantic validation is used to enforce the expected response structure before it is returned to the UI.

This is particularly useful for enterprise AI applications because downstream systems can reliably consume structured fields instead of attempting to parse arbitrary LLM text.

Example Output

For an overheating and vibration incident involving Pump-14, the system can produce:

Summary

Pump-14 is experiencing overheating and vibration spikes that have been observed in previous cycles.

Possible Causes
Coolant blockage
Bearing wear
Recommended Checks
Inspect the cooling line for blockages
Check coolant filter condition
Verify coolant circulation path
Inspect for bearing wear and shaft alignment
Confidence

Medium

Tools Used
search_sops
get_equipment_history
Sources
pump_overheating_sop.txt
vibration_response_sop.txt
equipment_history.json
